Z||000000||GeeDee86GT||12-22-2000||10:15 AM||||Have a chance for a cheap 88 Beretta coupe, 3.1 w/less than 5k miles (2nd motor) which was t-boned. I think it's an auto.  Is it worthwhile vs rebuilding my 2.8? Will the 3.1 bolt in? How much electronics must come with?  Thanks for your input.<P>Gary<BR>86 SE (v-6, 4 spd) <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/garby_d" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/garby_d</A> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||p4n1c||12-22-2000||10:43 AM||||The '88 Beretta was never offered with a 3.1, unless the previous owner popped it in(since it is the 2nd motor, this is entirely possible)?  It came standard with the 2.0L 4-cyl or you could option the 2.8l V6.  The GT and CL package were just looks upgrades.<P> <A HREF="http://www.beretta.net/model_info/1988.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.beretta.net/model_info/1988.htm</A> <P><BR>------------------<BR>p4n1c<BR>Come out swingin'<p>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 12-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black88GT||12-22-2000||11:45 AM||||It probably is a 3.1, because I know a lot of people are getting those Jasper engine replacements, in which they use the 3.1 over the 2.8.  <P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000003||GeeDee86GT||12-22-2000||07:15 PM||||I take it then, it is a direct bolt in.  Do you use the 2.8 ECM, etc? Baretta or Fiero Flywheel? I'll be using the 4 spd from an '84 Fiero.<P>Gary||||reg||2
Z||000004||Will||12-22-2000||08:54 PM||||'88 and later V6's used neutral balance flywheels. I don't know if the Iron Duck uses a neutral balance flywheel or not. That would be the only major impediment I see.<BR>     You may be in for some electronics work. I recently took apart an 87 or 88 Beretta to get a Getrag (I'm a collector  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) and it used a Mass Aairflow Sensor (MAF). It would be more work, but if your Beretta uses a MAF, you should transplant the ECM and do the wiring work necessary to use that system in your car. A MAF based ECM is much better able to work with modified engines than a speed-density system is.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||12-25-2000||05:20 AM||||I got the "How to Rebuild Your Chevy 60* V-6" book a little bit ago and saw that other 2.8's used the MAF system, and got to thinkin' what good it would do to swap it in.. any opinions?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||12-25-2000||07:18 PM||||This is how a speed-density system works:<BR>     The manifold absolute pressure (MAP) sensor tells the computer what the pressure behind the throttle valve is. The computer uses this input along with engine RPM to find the cell in the fuel map that determines injector pulse width for that throttle opening and engine speed. The fuel map is a table with RPM along the top and intake manifold pressure along the side; for a given RPM and pressure there is a number that determines how long the injectors stay open.<P>A mass-air flow system uses a MAF sensor to determine how much air the engine is ingesting. Knowing the injector flow rate and the mass of air going into the engine, the computer calculates the injector pulse width necessary maintain a desired AFR.<P>With a speed density system, any modification that changes (increases usually) the mass of air the engine can flow at a given mainfold pressure will require recalibration of the ECM because the engine's "total flow rate" is "built into" the fuel map.<BR>     With a MAF system, the ECM always knows the mass of air going into the engine and always knows how much fuel the engine needs to maintain the target AFR, regardless of modifications (within reason, of course).<BR>     Until recently, neither system paid any mind at all to the O2 sensor at full throttle. The LS6 in Z06 Corvettes (and the LS1, too?) does use the O2 sensor at WOT.||||reg||1
Z||000007||David Gamache||12-27-2000||11:23 AM||||I am in the process of picking up a 3.1 liter motor this weekend from a 92 pontiac  and have many of the same questions you do. I have read the threads from previous post and it seems that the 3.1L short block should be the same as the 2.8L but the 3.4 is a different creature. The 3.1L is internally balanced and the 85-87 V-6 fieros were not internally balanced so a balanced flywheel or flexplate is required for the swap. also, from what I have read, the heads and everything else should bolt to the short block but the fuel pressure regulator may need to be changed to the adjustable type and you may want to use the fuel injectors from the 3.1L. This should also mean that the electronics should remain in place from the 2.8L. The 3.1L uses a DIS system instead of a distributor but the 3.1L does have a capped opening for the distributor and even has the distributor hold down clamp. Th cam may or may not need to be changed. Since the 3.1L would have the added distributor and different heads from the fiero the fiero 2.8L cam may need to be installed in the 3.1L but I have not read any comments on this. The higher flow shorter heads of the fiero bolted to the 3.1L should produce more HP along with the longer stroke cam and the larger bore. At the very least, I understand the crank can be used from the 3.1L in the 2.8L with shorter flat pistons to create a stroker motor. If anyone out there has a completed project, and from all the threads I have read over the past year, someone should have a completed project by now please comment on this thread. In the mean time once I have the block in hand I should be able to confirm what is or is not necessary in complete detail. Good luck on the project and keep us posted. Thanks DG||||reg||1
